WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.930 --> 00:00:06.899
The world is pretty big and incredibly diverse,
so whenever you’re somewhere and you think

00:00:06.899 --> 00:00:12.870
‘it can’t get much hotter or wetter or
higher than this’; it probably can.

00:00:12.870 --> 00:00:23.520
So where are the undisputed champions: Where
are the most extreme places on earth?

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:26.230
First let’s look at the world’s hottest
place.

00:00:26.230 --> 00:00:30.480
Temperatures taken with a thermometer on weather
stations would have you believe that El Azizia

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:36.770
in Libya with it’s highest recorded temperature
of 58 °C or Death Valley in California with

00:00:36.770 --> 00:00:42.390
it’s record high reading of 56.7 °C are
the hottest places on Earth.

00:00:42.390 --> 00:00:47.880
But according to new satellite imagery data,
they’re not even serious contenders.

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:53.210
There is a salt desert in the east of Iran,
that’s about 480 kilometers long and 320

00:00:53.210 --> 00:01:00.220
wide, called Dasht-e Lut, which, as of recently,
is officially the hottest place on Earth.

00:01:00.220 --> 00:01:07.680
The surface of the sand here reaches a scorching
70.7 Celsius, that’s almost double the temperature

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:08.680
of your body.

00:01:08.680 --> 00:01:13.040
So, I guess that begs the question; who is
the poor scientist who had to sit out here

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:15.450
with a thermometer and take a reading every
day?

00:01:15.450 --> 00:01:17.869
I hope they had a fridge at least.

00:01:17.869 --> 00:01:24.189
Well, we know scientists can be a little crazy
sometimes, but no one is that crazy.

00:01:24.189 --> 00:01:30.170
The data actually comes from a NASA satellite
called AQUA that monitors, among other things,

00:01:30.170 --> 00:01:32.950
the heat radiation of the earth’s surface.

00:01:32.950 --> 00:01:40.049
From 2003 to 2010 the satellite would image
the entire planet every 1 to 2 days, giving

00:01:40.049 --> 00:01:43.790
us data on temperatures, clouds, winds and
much more.

00:01:43.790 --> 00:01:49.100
The local name for the desert is Gandom Beryan,
which means “Roasted Grain”.

00:01:49.100 --> 00:01:52.930
The story is that travelling farmers spilt
their seeds on the sand and within just a

00:01:52.930 --> 00:01:57.490
few days, it was scorched and ruined by the
heat before it could be collected.

00:01:57.490 --> 00:02:02.560
Not that you get a lot of people travelling
through, it’s certainly no picnic out there.

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:09.379
But people do come to see the beautiful, otherworldly
sandcastles in the western corner of the desert.

00:02:09.379 --> 00:02:14.749
The erosion has carved teetering towers that
make it seem like some lost, ancient city.

00:02:14.749 --> 00:02:19.739
Right, so if that got you feeling a little
hot under the collar, let’s all cool off

00:02:19.739 --> 00:02:23.999
by heading in the opposite direction; the
coldest place on earth.

00:02:23.999 --> 00:02:33.249
The lowest recorded temperature is -89.2 °C,
taken in 1983 at the Sovient Vostok Station.

00:02:33.249 --> 00:02:37.800
But the station was just there to make these
measurements, so, you know, the scientist

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:44.180
were being paid to be there, in cold hard
cash – very cold hard cash.

00:02:44.180 --> 00:02:49.870
But imagine if your whole town CHOSE to live
like this.

00:02:49.870 --> 00:02:57.470
Oymyakon is the coldest permanent settlement
on Earth, with some 500 people living in freezing

00:02:57.470 --> 00:03:00.469
temperatures, up in the North-Eastern tip
of Russia.

00:03:00.469 --> 00:03:09.219
In winter it has got as low as −67.7 °C
and typically averages -45 C (-49 F) in December

00:03:09.219 --> 00:03:10.579
and January.

00:03:10.579 --> 00:03:16.400
The craziest thing is that, unlike Antarctica
where you’re lucky if you get above -30

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:26.079
Celcius, even in summer, Oymyakon has hit
+ 34.6 °C in July, and normally averages

00:03:26.079 --> 00:03:28.900
around 20 °C for the summer months.

00:03:28.900 --> 00:03:34.109
Imagine trying to buy a yearly wardrobe that
covered a difference of 100 Celcius!

00:03:34.109 --> 00:03:39.849
Enough about temperature, we’re moving on
to new heights, or just really high heights.

00:03:39.849 --> 00:03:43.099
So what is the world’s highest point?

00:03:43.099 --> 00:03:46.129
Now this seems like quite an obvious one,
right?

00:03:46.129 --> 00:03:51.859
The highest point must be the highest mountain;
which is, yep, that’s right; Mount Everest

00:03:51.859 --> 00:03:53.629
in the Himalayas.

00:03:53.629 --> 00:03:59.030
But when we say “the highest mountain”,
what do we really mean?

00:03:59.030 --> 00:04:05.139
We mean Everest’s peak is 8,848 m above
sea level, and no other mountain can match

00:04:05.139 --> 00:04:06.180
that.

00:04:06.180 --> 00:04:10.949
If you measure from the base to the peak,
which is quite hard to do because there isn’t

00:04:10.949 --> 00:04:16.150
some nice red line that says “the mountain
starts here,” then actually the tallest

00:04:16.150 --> 00:04:21.280
mountain is Mauna Kea, at a massive 10,203
m.

00:04:21.280 --> 00:04:28.530
But it’s deep in the Pacific Ocean, the
first 6,000 metres of Mauna Kea are underwater,

00:04:28.530 --> 00:04:32.190
so it doesn’t really help us find the world’s
highest point.

00:04:32.190 --> 00:04:34.010
Let me introduce; Chimborazo.

00:04:34.010 --> 00:04:41.409
Okay, I know it’s only 6,267 m above sea
level, but here’s the twist; its peak is

00:04:41.409 --> 00:04:45.740
the furthest away from the center of the earth
of any mountain.

00:04:45.740 --> 00:04:50.020
You know that globe you had in geography class
that was a perfect sphere?

00:04:50.020 --> 00:04:54.270
Well the earth isn’t actually like that,
it bulges around the equator, so the land,

00:04:54.270 --> 00:05:00.800
and the sea level, are actually a few kilometers
higher at these points than anywhere else.

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:06.340
Since Chimborazo is in Ecuador, named for
being right on the equator, it gets this little

00:05:06.340 --> 00:05:15.729
boost from the Earth’s bulge, so its 6,384.4
km from the center of the Earth, which beats

00:05:15.729 --> 00:05:21.210
Everest at 6,382.3 km from the Earth’s center.

00:05:21.210 --> 00:05:23.849
In your face Everest, you midget.

00:05:23.849 --> 00:05:29.020
So that’s the highest point, but what about
the lowest.

00:05:29.020 --> 00:05:31.790
The lowest place on the earth’s surface
is The Dead Sea.

00:05:31.790 --> 00:05:37.400
It’s not really a sea, it’s more a really
salty lake, 50 km long and 15 wide, and it

00:05:37.400 --> 00:05:43.289
lies almost half a kilometer below sea level,
429 metres to be exact.

00:05:43.289 --> 00:05:48.020
It gets its name from the almost total lack
of aquatic life there; you’re more likely

00:05:48.020 --> 00:05:50.780
to find chips in it than fish.

00:05:50.780 --> 00:05:55.689
Its salt quantity is so high that swimmers
just float on the surface, like a human inflatable.

00:05:55.689 --> 00:06:00.919
But if we don’t stick to the earth’s surface,
we can find places much deeper.

00:06:00.919 --> 00:06:06.449
In the Pacific Ocean, just south of Japan,
there is Mariana’s Trench and its lowest

00:06:06.449 --> 00:06:13.099
point is called Challenger Deep and bottoms
out at 10,916 m below sea level.

00:06:13.099 --> 00:06:17.780
Here the pressure reaches over 1,000 times
what it is at the surface.

00:06:17.780 --> 00:06:22.419
So unless you happen to be an invertebrate
or other soft-bodied organism, you would probably

00:06:22.419 --> 00:06:27.490
end up as soup under the immense pressure,
that deep underwater.

00:06:27.490 --> 00:06:31.530
Have you ever had that feeling when you’re
at the top of a really high building or say,

00:06:31.530 --> 00:06:35.689
a waterfall and you wonder what it would feel
like if you jumped?

00:06:35.689 --> 00:06:37.160
Well you’re not alone.

00:06:37.160 --> 00:06:41.622
It’s often called the “High-Place Phenomenon”
and some psychologists thinks it’s your

00:06:41.622 --> 00:06:46.910
brain getting confused by the feeling of dizziness
and vertigo, even though no realistic threat

00:06:46.910 --> 00:06:48.789
is being posed.

00:06:48.789 --> 00:06:53.350
So imagine what it would feel like if you
took a trip to the top of Mount Thor, the

00:06:53.350 --> 00:06:56.250
world’s greatest vertical drop.

00:06:56.250 --> 00:07:03.860
This gigantic cliff face can be found on Baffin
Island in Canada and boasts a 1,250 m vertical

00:07:03.860 --> 00:07:04.860
drop.

00:07:04.860 --> 00:07:10.080
It would take the average person over 15 seconds
to reach the bottom if they jumped off it.

00:07:10.080 --> 00:07:14.810
And because the world is a crazy place, people
do jump off it.

00:07:14.810 --> 00:07:19.340
In fact, Baffin Island has become something
of a hotspot for Base Jumpers who brave the

00:07:19.340 --> 00:07:23.930
Arctic conditions and Polar Bears to hop in
a wingsuit and scream “Geronimo” as they

00:07:23.930 --> 00:07:28.939
take a wild plunge from Thor’s Peak
The thought of doing one of those jumps makes

00:07:28.939 --> 00:07:31.380
my mouth go dry.

00:07:31.380 --> 00:07:34.069
Do you know where else would do that?

00:07:34.069 --> 00:07:36.210
The driest place on earth.

00:07:36.210 --> 00:07:41.919
Down the west coast of South America, tucked
behind the Andes, lies a 1,000 km strip called

00:07:41.919 --> 00:07:43.199
The Atacama Desert.

00:07:43.199 --> 00:07:49.889
It’s so dry that it only receives around
15 mm of rain every year and as little as

00:07:49.889 --> 00:07:52.080
1 mm in some parts.

00:07:52.080 --> 00:07:56.510
That means if you spit on the ground, you’re
giving it more moisture than it’ll see all

00:07:56.510 --> 00:07:57.510
year.

00:07:57.510 --> 00:08:01.870
This isn’t just the recent recordings either;
geologists believe that it’s been like this

00:08:01.870 --> 00:08:07.879
for some 200 million years, so, you never
know, an Argentinosaurus could have come along

00:08:07.879 --> 00:08:10.860
and dribbled on the exact same dry spot.

00:08:10.860 --> 00:08:16.039
Surprisingly, the land isn’t completely
barren and some plants and animals do exist,

00:08:16.039 --> 00:08:17.569
mainly around the edges of the desert.

00:08:17.569 --> 00:08:22.749
And some years, if you do enough rain dances
and manage to get over the usual 15 mm of

00:08:22.749 --> 00:08:26.469
precipitation, then huge areas of the desert
burst into colour.

00:08:26.469 --> 00:08:32.200
A variety of seeds and bulbs lay dormant through
the dry years but come alive in a visual symphony

00:08:32.200 --> 00:08:35.419
when the rains do finally return.

00:08:35.419 --> 00:08:36.899
When discussing dry places.

00:08:36.899 --> 00:08:40.539
We should also mention The Dry Valleys in
Antarctica.

00:08:40.539 --> 00:08:47.890
In an enormous continent of whiteness, these
are a strange sight; permanently ice free

00:08:47.890 --> 00:08:48.890
valleys.

00:08:48.890 --> 00:08:53.149
Well, they do have a very small number of
frozen lakes but it’s mainly just harsh

00:08:53.149 --> 00:08:54.500
grey looking dirt.

00:08:54.500 --> 00:08:58.100
The reason it’s so dry is due to katabatic
winds.

00:08:58.100 --> 00:09:02.740
The surrounding mountains block the flowing
ice and then, when cold air comes over the

00:09:02.740 --> 00:09:09.070
top, the gravity pulls it downwards, making
it rip through the valleys at up to 320 kilometers

00:09:09.070 --> 00:09:10.440
per hour.

00:09:10.440 --> 00:09:14.620
This heats the air and evaporates all the
ice and snow.

00:09:14.620 --> 00:09:19.680
This wind also whips up tiny stones and sandblasts
the boulders into these beautiful carvings

00:09:19.680 --> 00:09:21.890
called “vertifacts” that look like enormous
dinosaur skeletons.

00:09:21.890 --> 00:09:25.490
It gives it the feeling of being in an alien
world.

00:09:25.490 --> 00:09:32.899
And in fact, because of their alien-like features,
both the Dry Valleys and the Atacama Desert

00:09:32.899 --> 00:09:39.699
are used to test various NASA projects, such
as the rovers for Mars and one of Jupiter’s

00:09:39.699 --> 00:09:40.880
moons, Europa.

00:09:40.880 --> 00:09:47.100
If you want to escape these dry sci-fi landscapes,
where better than the world’s wettest place?

00:09:47.100 --> 00:09:52.421
Mawsynram, a village in north-eastern India,
holds’ the Guinness World Record as the

00:09:52.421 --> 00:09:59.260
world’s wettest place with a current average
of 11,872 mm per year.

00:09:59.260 --> 00:10:04.850
To give you some perspective, that’s ten
times what rainy old England normally averages.

00:10:04.850 --> 00:10:09.199
The causes of all this rain are related to
the Khasi Hills, which are placed right in

00:10:09.199 --> 00:10:12.350
the path of airflow that comes from the Bay
of Bengal.

00:10:12.350 --> 00:10:17.120
One key factor is that the air coming over
the hills creates uplift and cooling which

00:10:17.120 --> 00:10:21.399
increases the condensation, so it’s like
you’re constantly pumping the clouds full

00:10:21.399 --> 00:10:22.900
of more water.

00:10:22.900 --> 00:10:27.550
The title of “world’s wettest place”
is actually contested by two towns in Columbia;

00:10:27.550 --> 00:10:29.769
Lloro and Puerto Lopez.

00:10:29.769 --> 00:10:37.690
Lloro had an average of 13,299 mm of rainfall,
for a few years in the 1950s but the equipment

00:10:37.690 --> 00:10:41.770
was pretty outdated so we don’t know how
much we can trust these figures.

00:10:41.770 --> 00:10:48.880
As for Puerto Lopez, although it has decent
records to show a 12,892 mm precipitation

00:10:48.880 --> 00:10:54.190
average, there are some months missing in
the middle of 50 years of data so unfortunately

00:10:54.190 --> 00:10:56.460
they cannot claim the title.

00:10:56.460 --> 00:11:00.949
Seems a bit harsh but hey, if you’re sitting
inside all day, you should have time to do

00:11:00.949 --> 00:11:03.470
your filing properly, right?

00:11:03.470 --> 00:11:07.269
If you’ve forgotten your umbrella, maybe
we should look for somewhere else for you

00:11:07.269 --> 00:11:08.430
to go.

00:11:08.430 --> 00:11:11.830
How about the world’s highest permanent
settlement?

00:11:11.830 --> 00:11:15.490
This honour goes to La Rinconada, in the Peruvian
Andes.

00:11:15.490 --> 00:11:20.589
It sits at the bottom of the “The Sleeping
Beauty” Glacier and is a whopping 5,100

00:11:20.589 --> 00:11:22.800
m above sea level.

00:11:22.800 --> 00:11:28.180
It has a pretty stable climate and is typically
a degree or two above freezing all year round.

00:11:28.180 --> 00:11:31.959
So what’s the appeal for the 50,000 or so
inhabitants?

00:11:31.959 --> 00:11:35.220
It’s one of mankind’s old loves; gold!

00:11:35.220 --> 00:11:41.000
There are gold mines nearby and this is where
almost all the economy comes from.

00:11:41.000 --> 00:11:44.050
But the way workers are paid is very strange.

00:11:44.050 --> 00:11:46.590
Most are part of the cachorreo system.

00:11:46.590 --> 00:11:52.389
This means they go 30 days unpaid and then
on the 31st day, they can take as much ore

00:11:52.389 --> 00:11:56.240
as they can carry and hope that they find
gold in it.

00:11:56.240 --> 00:12:00.410
It’s a complete lottery of course but it’s
common practice to pocket a promising looking

00:12:00.410 --> 00:12:04.139
stone even when it’s not a cachorreo day.

00:12:04.139 --> 00:12:08.110
As well as the peculiar payment system, the
city is not the safest place either.

00:12:08.110 --> 00:12:12.769
There is no plumbing or sanitation system
and since mercury is used in the gold refinement

00:12:12.769 --> 00:12:15.120
process, the water is pretty contaminated.

00:12:15.120 --> 00:12:20.730
But hey, who needs toilets and non-toxic water
if you’ve got yourself some shiny shiny

00:12:20.730 --> 00:12:21.730
gold.

00:12:21.730 --> 00:12:27.000
And finally, if you really want to get away
from it all, you need to head down to the

00:12:27.000 --> 00:12:30.149
most remote inhabited place on Earth.

00:12:30.149 --> 00:12:35.560
Around 300 people live on the Island of Tristan
da Cunha, which is 2,000 kilometres from its

00:12:35.560 --> 00:12:39.589
nearest neighbours on the island of St Helena;
that’s a long way to paddle if you need

00:12:39.589 --> 00:12:41.570
to tell them to turn the music down.

00:12:41.570 --> 00:12:47.120
The nearest continental land is South Africa,
some 2,400 kilometres away.

00:12:47.120 --> 00:12:52.600
It’s actually a small cluster of islands;
one is uninhabitable, some are just wildlife

00:12:52.600 --> 00:12:56.279
reserves, but the main island where people
live is 98km2.

00:12:56.279 --> 00:13:00.149
And these people are members of the British
Commonwealth.

00:13:00.149 --> 00:13:06.019
The island was discovered by the Portuguese
admiral Tristão da Cunha, in 1506 but was

00:13:06.019 --> 00:13:10.980
only used as a stop off point for merchants
and whalers until the early 19th century.

00:13:10.980 --> 00:13:16.880
The British declared it part of its empire
in 1816, due to two main concerns.

00:13:16.880 --> 00:13:21.570
Firstly, during the war with the United States,
the US used it to launch attacks on British

00:13:21.570 --> 00:13:23.029
merchant fleets.

00:13:23.029 --> 00:13:28.339
And secondly, it was to stop Napoleon from
escaping from St Helena.

00:13:28.339 --> 00:13:34.430
Napoleon had already escaped once before,
from a prison on Elba, near Italy, so after

00:13:34.430 --> 00:13:39.210
the battle of Waterloo, the British took no
chances and sent him as far away as they could

00:13:39.210 --> 00:13:40.540
from anyone.

00:13:40.540 --> 00:13:46.019
The navy on Tristan Da Cunha would patrol
the waters and look out for any French attack.

00:13:46.019 --> 00:13:50.839
Once Napoleon died, from arsenic poisoning
he got from his wallpaper, the fleet decided

00:13:50.839 --> 00:13:55.199
to stay and so the island has been inhabited
ever since.

00:13:55.199 --> 00:14:00.589
It was briefly evacuated in 1961 when the
island’s volcano, Queen Mary’s peak, erupted.

00:14:00.589 --> 00:14:05.959
They have their own economy, largely from
renting out their outland fishing waters.

00:14:05.959 --> 00:14:10.819
They also sell unique coins and postage stamps
that are something of a collector’s item.

00:14:10.819 --> 00:14:15.790
Since it’s hard to trade when you live 2,000
kilometres away from everyone, most of their

00:14:15.790 --> 00:14:20.920
supplies come eight to nine times a year from
South African fishing boats.

00:14:20.920 --> 00:14:23.930
It makes ordering off Amazon a bit of slow
process.

00:14:23.930 --> 00:14:27.870
They do have internet access now though, since
an internet café opened up in 2006.

00:14:27.870 --> 00:14:33.970
But the internet is so slow on the island
that when someone wants to Skype a relative

00:14:33.970 --> 00:14:40.029
the entire island has to turn off their internet
access to free up bandwidth.

00:14:40.029 --> 00:14:46.220
The world certainly is full of extreme places
that push the limits and boggle the mind,

00:14:46.220 --> 00:14:51.100
but unless you want to be incredibly hot,
perishingly cold, unnervingly high, incredibly

00:14:51.100 --> 00:14:56.910
dehydrated, completely soaked, or just really
really far from anyone, then you’re best

00:14:56.910 --> 00:15:25.270
of staying right where you are.

